00:24:30.835 --> 00:24:31.668
how do you think it's going to change

00:24:31.668 --> 00:24:32.820
for this coming school year?

00:24:32.820 --> 00:24:34.120
Because that's what you know,

00:24:34.120 --> 00:24:35.310
I don't think a lot of
folks have had a chance

00:24:35.310 --> 00:24:36.817
to even think about that.

00:24:36.817 --> 00:24:39.760
- Yeah I think it's a
really tough question

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:42.530
that a lot of people are
grappling with everywhere

00:24:42.530 --> 00:24:45.110
from, you know, the
state level to districts

00:24:45.110 --> 00:24:46.392
to the individual classroom.

00:24:46.392 --> 00:24:49.440
And I think the biggest
challenge this year is, you know,

00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:52.210
teachers are used to dealing
with the summer slide

00:24:52.210 --> 00:24:55.440
or learning loss in the US but
now we have this, you know,

00:24:55.440 --> 00:24:58.530
extended time where students
haven't been you know,

00:24:58.530 --> 00:25:00.120
in the classroom, per se.

00:25:00.120 --> 00:25:02.762
I think the biggest challenge
for teachers is going to be,

00:25:02.762 --> 00:25:06.950
how do I support students in
learning what they missed,

00:25:06.950 --> 00:25:08.538
while also not letting them fall behind

00:25:08.538 --> 00:25:10.950
from what they're supposed
to learn on grade level.

00:25:10.950 --> 00:25:13.810
And I think some sort of
interleaving of, you know,

00:25:13.810 --> 00:25:16.480
previous skills and
current skills is gonna be

00:25:16.480 --> 00:25:18.210
even more prominent this year

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:19.913
than we've seen in years prior.

00:25:21.319 --> 00:25:24.140
- Yeah, and I'll just
add to that, you know,

00:25:24.140 --> 00:25:27.130
your traditional curricula
are based on this notion

00:25:27.130 --> 00:25:28.970
of you have five class periods a week

00:25:28.970 --> 00:25:31.530
that are 55 minutes long.

00:25:31.530 --> 00:25:33.730
And that that kind of stays
consistently through the year

00:25:33.730 --> 00:25:35.250
and the teacher can check your homework

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:37.910
and then give kind of a
well thought out lesson.

00:25:37.910 --> 00:25:39.850
And obviously, in this COVID world,

00:25:39.850 --> 00:25:41.580
whatever permutation it takes, you know,

00:25:41.580 --> 00:25:44.440
the permutation seem to
be skewing more towards

00:25:44.440 --> 00:25:46.470
what we saw in the spring because COVID

00:25:46.470 --> 00:25:48.293
only picking back up.

00:25:49.210 --> 00:25:50.820
You're not going to be able to do that.

00:25:50.820 --> 00:25:52.923
You know, what the best
case that we've been seeing

00:25:52.923 --> 00:25:57.123
is a teacher's leaning on Khan Academy,

00:25:57.123 --> 00:26:00.700
both with the course mastery
and by making assignment

00:26:00.700 --> 00:26:02.390
so that they can keep the track keep track

00:26:02.390 --> 00:26:04.253
of what students are doing
at their own time and pace,

00:26:04.253 --> 00:26:08.990
and then doing what's called
three synchronous sessions

00:26:08.990 --> 00:26:10.359
over video conference a week,

00:26:10.359 --> 00:26:13.550
maybe two of those sessions
are where teachers focus on

00:26:13.550 --> 00:26:17.800
particular problem types
that they saw their students

00:26:17.800 --> 00:26:19.590
have particular difficulty in,

00:26:19.590 --> 00:26:21.370
and then maybe another
session where they just answer

00:26:21.370 --> 00:26:22.460
any questions students have.

00:26:22.460 --> 00:26:24.940
And so I think in this new framework,

00:26:24.940 --> 00:26:26.830
which is very likely to
be at least the first half

00:26:26.830 --> 00:26:27.663
of the school year,

00:26:27.663 --> 00:26:29.203
